```js
// Resolves a single ball-vs-brick collision: flips the correct velocity
// component and pushes the ball out. Returns whether a bounce occurred.
export function bounceOff(ball, brick) {
  // compute penetration on each axis
  const rightPen = (ball.x + ball.r) - brick.x;
  const leftPen = (brick.x + brick.w) - (ball.x - ball.r);
  const minX = Math.min(rightPen, leftPen);

  const bottomPen = (ball.y + ball.r) - brick.y;
  const topPen = (brick.y + brick.h) - (ball.y - ball.r);
  const minY = Math.min(bottomPen, topPen);

  if (minX < minY) {
    // side hit
    if (rightPen < leftPen) {
      ball.vx = -Math.abs(ball.vx);
      ball.x = brick.x - ball.r;
    } else {
      ball.vx = Math.abs(ball.vx);
      ball.x = brick.x + brick.w + ball.r;
    }
  } else if (minY < minX) {
    // top/bottom hit
    if (bottomPen < topPen) {
      ball.vy = -Math.abs(ball.vy);
      ball.y = brick.y - ball.r;
    } else {
      ball.vy = Math.abs(ball.vy);
      ball.y = brick.y + brick.h + ball.r;
    }
  } else {
    // corner: flip both
    if (rightPen < leftPen) {
      ball.vx = -Math.abs(ball.vx);
      ball.x = brick.x - ball.r;
    } else {
      ball.vx = Math.abs(ball.vx);
      ball.x = brick.x + brick.w + ball.r;
    }
    if (bottomPen < topPen) {
      ball.vy = -Math.abs(ball.vy);
      ball.y = brick.y - ball.r;
    } else {
      ball.vy = Math.abs(ball.vy);
      ball.y = brick.y + brick.h + ball.r;
    }
  }
  return true;
}
```

Now the "neighbours" issue: seams overlap two bricks in the same pass. The bounce must apply exactly once. So I need to ensure we don't bounce off a brick twice. 

Consider: the ball overlaps two adjacent bricks A and B on the same row. When we bounce the ball and push it out, it should be pushed out of both. But if we process brick A first, push the ball to the top of brick A, then brick B still overlaps. We re-process B, but now since ball is above A and B... Hmm.

Actually the approach "bounce each brick but only apply exact once" means: we need to avoid double-flipping. The issue says "the bounce must apply exactly once (`vy = -vy` applied twice cancels out and the ball goes through)".

The trick: When two bricks overlap due to a seam, applying a bounce to the first brick flips vy and pushes the ball. Applying bounce to the second brick would also flip vy again (cancelling) and push it differently. To avoid this, once we bounce off a set of bricks in a pass, we mark that this collision pass is done.

A robust approach: process the sweep segment; for the ball at its position, find which bricks it overlaps. Flip the appropriate component(s) based on the single deepest axis of penetration across ALL overlapping bricks, and push out once. But that's complex.

Simpler and effective approach often used: In one frame, resolve the ball against the brick once. Since the bounce pushes the ball out entirely (ball's box no longer overlaps the brick), a *single* bounce handles the seam because after pushing out the first brick, the ball moves to the top edge which is above both adjacent bricks on the same row.

Wait but the two seam bricks are side-by-side (same row). If ball hits the top face of the seam, both A and B overlap. Processing A: top hit → push ball above A, flip vy. Now ball is completely above A. Is it above B too? B is on the same row as A, so yes, ball is above B's top edge too. So when processing B, the ball no longer overlaps B (ball's bottom is at/above A.y, and B.y == A.y). So B won't trigger a bounce. 

But what if ball hits the side of a seam column? Two bricks stacked vertically (A above B). If ball hits from side, it overlaps both A and B's side faces. Processing A: side hit → push ball to left of A, flip vx. Now ball at left of A.x. B has same x as A, so ball is also left of B. B no longer overlaps. Good.

So the key insight: pushing the ball completely out of the first brick also gets it out of its seam neighbor (since they share the face the ball is pushed to). Therefore, if we iterate and stop processing once we've bounced (applied once), we're fine. But we still need to be careful about ordering and multiple distinct bricks not in the same seam.

